To find the number of elements in \( K^\times \) such that the norm \( N_{K/k}(x) = 1 \), we start by noting that \( K = \mathbb{F}_{2^{20}} \) is a Galois extension of \( k = \mathbb{F}_{2^{10}} \) of degree 2. The Frobenius automorphism \( \sigma \) is given by \( \sigma(x) = x^{2^{10}} \).

The norm map \( N_{K/k}(x) = \sigma(x)x \) translates to \( N_{K/k}(x) = x \cdot x^{2^{10}} = x^{1 + 2^{10}} = x^{1025} \). We need to find the number of elements \( x \in K^\times \) such that \( x^{1025} = 1 \).

The multiplicative group \( K^\times \) is cyclic of order \( |\mathbb{F}_{2^{20}}^\times| = 2^{20} - 1 \). The number of solutions to the equation \( x^n = 1 \) in a cyclic group of order \( m \) is \( \gcd(n, m) \). Here, \( n = 1025 \) and \( m = 2^{20} - 1 \).

First, factorize \( 2^{20} - 1 \):
\[
2^{20} - 1 = (2^{10})^2 - 1 = (2^{10} - 1)(2^{10} + 1) = 1023 \times 1025
\]
Since \( 1025 \) is a factor of \( 2^{20} - 1 \), we have \( \gcd(1025, 2^{20} - 1) = 1025 \). Therefore, the number of solutions is \( 1025 \).

Alternatively, using the properties of the norm map, we know it is a group homomorphism \( N_{K/k}: K^\times \to k^\times \) with kernel size \( \frac{|K^\times|}{|k^\times|} = \frac{(2^{20} - 1)}{(2^{10} - 1)} = \frac{(2^{10} - 1)(2^{10} + 1)}{2^{10} - 1} = 2^{10} + 1 = 1025 \).

Thus, the number of elements in \( K^\times \) satisfying \( N_{K/k}(x) = 1 \) is \(\boxed{1025}\).<|im_end|>
