To find the number of possible values of \( S \), the sum of a 90-element subset of \(\{1, 2, 3, \ldots, 100\}\), we first note that the sum \( S \) can be expressed as the total sum of all elements from 1 to 100 minus the sum of the 10-element complementary subset. 

The total sum of all numbers from 1 to 100 is:
\[
\frac{100 \cdot 101}{2} = 5050
\]

Let \( T \) be the sum of the 10-element complementary subset. The possible values of \( S \) range from \( 5050 - T_{\text{max}} \) to \( 5050 - T_{\text{min}} \), where \( T_{\text{min}} \) is the minimum sum of a 10-element subset and \( T_{\text{max}} \) is the maximum sum of a 10-element subset.

The minimum sum \( T_{\text{min}} \) occurs when the subset is \(\{1, 2, \ldots, 10\}\):
\[
T_{\text{min}} = \frac{10 \cdot 11}{2} = 55
\]

The maximum sum \( T_{\text{max}} \) occurs when the subset is \(\{91, 92, \ldots, 100\}\):
\[
T_{\text{max}} = \frac{(91 + 100) \cdot 10}{2} = \frac{191 \cdot 10}{2} = 955
\]

The range of possible values for \( T \) is from 55 to 955. Since every integer in this range is achievable by some subset of size 10 (verified through examples and logical reasoning), the number of possible values for \( T \) is:
\[
955 - 55 + 1 = 901
\]

Thus, the number of possible values for \( S \) (the sum of the 90-element subset) is also 901. 

\[
\boxed{901}
\]<|im_end|>
