To evaluate the limit:
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4}\frac{n\cos(x)}{n^2x^2+1}\,dx
\]
we start by making a substitution to simplify the integrand. Let \( t = nx \), so \( x = \frac{t}{n} \) and \( dx = \frac{dt}{n} \). The integral transforms as follows:
\[
\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \frac{n\cos(x)}{n^2x^2 + 1} \, dx = \int_{-n\pi/4}^{n\pi/4} \frac{n\cos\left(\frac{t}{n}\right)}{t^2 + 1} \cdot \frac{dt}{n} = \int_{-n\pi/4}^{n\pi/4} \frac{\cos\left(\frac{t}{n}\right)}{t^2 + 1} \, dt
\]

As \( n \to \infty \), the interval \([-n\pi/4, n\pi/4]\) expands to cover the entire real line. For each fixed \( t \), \( \cos\left(\frac{t}{n}\right) \) approaches 1 because \( \frac{t}{n} \) becomes negligible. By the Dominated Convergence Theorem, we can interchange the limit and the integral:
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty} \int_{-n\pi/4}^{n\pi/4} \frac{\cos\left(\frac{t}{n}\right)}{t^2 + 1} \, dt = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{1}{t^2 + 1} \, dt
\]

The integral \(\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{1}{t^2 + 1} \, dt\) is a standard result which evaluates to \( \pi \). Therefore, the limit of the original integral is:
\[
\boxed{\pi}
\]<|im_end|>
