To determine the ratio of the area of square \( WXYZ \) to the area of square \( ABCD \), we start by placing square \( ABCD \) on a coordinate system with vertices \( A(0,0) \), \( B(1,0) \), \( C(1,1) \), and \( D(0,1) \). An interior point \( H \) with coordinates \( (h, k) \) divides the square into four triangles: \( AHD \), \( AHB \), \( BHC \), and \( CHD \).

The centroids \( W \), \( X \), \( Y \), and \( Z \) of these triangles are calculated as follows:
- Centroid \( W \) (of \( AHD \)): \( \left( \frac{h}{3}, \frac{k + 1}{3} \right) \)
- Centroid \( X \) (of \( AHB \)): \( \left( \frac{h + 1}{3}, \frac{k}{3} \right) \)
- Centroid \( Y \) (of \( BHC \)): \( \left( \frac{h + 2}{3}, \frac{k + 1}{3} \right) \)
- Centroid \( Z \) (of \( CHD \)): \( \left( \frac{h + 1}{3}, \frac{k + 2}{3} \right) \)

We compute the vectors between consecutive centroids:
- Vector \( \overrightarrow{WX} \): \( \left( \frac{1}{3}, -\frac{1}{3} \right) \)
- Vector \( \overrightarrow{XY} \): \( \left( \frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{3} \right) \)
- Vector \( \overrightarrow{YZ} \): \( \left( -\frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{3} \right) \)
- Vector \( \overrightarrow{ZW} \): \( \left( -\frac{1}{3}, -\frac{1}{3} \right) \)

These vectors form the sides of a square, each with length \( \frac{\sqrt{2}}{3} \). The area of a square with side length \( \frac{\sqrt{2}}{3} \) is \( \left( \frac{\sqrt{2}}{3} \right)^2 = \frac{2}{9} \).

Since the vectors between the centroids are independent of the position of \( H \), the area of square \( WXYZ \) is consistently \( \frac{2}{9} \) of the area of square \( ABCD \).

Thus, the ratio of the area of square \( WXYZ \) to the area of square \( ABCD \) is \(\boxed{\dfrac{2}{9}}\).<|im_end|>
